--- out/runbook.md (600)
# Example network: runbook

Made by yubikey-turnkey <VERSION> from `site.toml`. Three servers: east1, east2, west.
east1 is the usual primary of the database; west becomes the primary only when the other two can't.

## 1. Before you start

- Each server: Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, its host name (`hostname -s`) as in
  `site.toml`, its own IPv4 and IPv6 addresses on an interface, the clock
  synchronized, and `apt-get` working.
- In each server's folder, next to `setup.sh`: Radiator's packages from
  Radiator Software (`radiator_4.30-1_all.deb` and
  `radiator-radius-utilxs_*.deb`), and `radiator-yubikey_<VERSION>_all.deb`
  with its `install.sh`; or run yubikey-turnkey again with `--packages DIR`.
- Between the servers, and from the admin subnets, SSH and the ports in
  `site.toml`'s comments must not be blocked by firewalls on the way.
- `backup.crt` is the backups' certificate: its private key is on none of
  the servers, and you keep it where you keep the backups.

## 2. Copy each folder to its server

Each folder holds secrets: copy it as root, readable by root only, for
example:

```bash
scp -r east1/ root@east1.example.com:/root/turnkey
scp -r east2/ root@east2.example.com:/root/turnkey
scp -r west/ root@west.example.com:/root/turnkey
```

## 3. Check each server

On each server, change nothing, and fix what it reports:

```bash
sudo /root/turnkey/setup.sh --check
```

## 4. Set up all three

Run setup.sh on all three, at about the same time (within 30 minutes of
each other): etcd starts only when two servers run it, the other servers
wait for east1's database, and each tells you what it waits for.

```bash
sudo /root/turnkey/setup.sh
```

east1 creates the database, with the anycast check's keys and
service accounts and the groups in `site.toml`. Each server ends with a
report: the cluster, the anycast check, and the BGP sessions, which come up
once the routers are configured (step 5). If setup.sh stops, fix the cause
and run it again: it goes on where it stopped. Run it again whenever
yubikey-turnkey changed the folder: it changes only what differs.

## 5. The routers

As in `routers.md`. Each server advertises the anycast addresses once its
BGP sessions are up and its check passes.

## 6. The admins' machines

The admins manage the YubiKeys, accounts and groups with `yubikey-db.sh`
(from the radiator-yubikey release, with `psql` from postgresql-client).
From `admin/`: `ca.crt` as `~/.yubikey-ca.crt`, your line of `pgpass` in
`~/.pgpass` (mode 0600), and `. ./pg.env`. The servers' names must resolve
there, to their IPv4 addresses. Then add the YubiKeys and put the accounts
in groups, as in the guide:

```bash
cd /opt/radiator-yubikey        # or the release's folder
./yubikey-db.sh import users.csv
./yubikey-db.sh member add netadmins alice
```

## 7. The devices

As in `devices.md`: the anycast addresses as their RADIUS and TACACS+
servers, with their secret and key.

## 8. Check

On each server: every line `ok` (the guide's "Check a turn-key server"
says what the others mean, and how monitoring can run it).

```bash
sudo /opt/radiator-yubikey/yubikey-turnkey-setup.sh --status
```

## 9. Backups

Each server dumps the database every day at about 03:00, encrypted to the
backups' certificate, and keeps 7: `/var/backups/yubikey-db.cms.0` (the
newest) to `.6`, readable by the group adm. Fetch them to where you
keep backups, and now and then check that one decrypts and reads back
whole, on your machine:

```bash
scp east1.example.com:/var/backups/yubikey-db.cms.0 .
yubikey-backup.sh check --key backup.key yubikey-db.cms.0
```

To restore one, see the guide's "Turn-key backups".

## A server lost

Set up a new one with the same name and addresses (as in step 1), copy
its folder (step 2), and run setup.sh on it alone, while the other two
run: it joins them again, and its anycast check goes on with its key from
the database. To start a broken server over in place instead: `setup.sh
--reset NAME` on it, then setup.sh. If it was east1, it is a standby
then: the guide's "Rebuild a turn-key server" says how to move the
primary back.

## Two servers lost

Only when both are lost for good, not cut off: on the third, `setup.sh
--alone` makes it the cluster alone (it asks first). Then set up the
other two again as above, and run setup.sh on both; the guide's "Two
servers lost" says the rest.

## Keep

`secrets.toml` and this folder hold every password and key: keep them, and
nothing else, where only you can read them. Each server keeps a copy of
its own in `/etc/yubikey-turnkey`.

`secrets.toml.cms` is `secrets.toml` encrypted to the backups'
certificate, and each server keeps it as
`/var/backups/yubikey-turnkey-secrets.toml.cms`: with the private key,
`yubikey-backup.sh decrypt` gives back `secrets.toml`.

--- out/routers.md (600)
# Example network: routers

Made by yubikey-turnkey <VERSION> from `site.toml`.

Each site's router (or both, where a site has two) has a BGP session with
its server over IPv4 and one over IPv6, with BFD. The server advertises
the anycast addresses while its Radiator logs users in, and withdraws
them when it can't:

- IPv4: `192.0.2.153/32`
- IPv6: `2001:db8:153::153/128`

Each router:

- accepts only these two routes from its server, at most one per session,
  and sends it nothing (the server ignores anything it sends);
- runs BFD on both sessions, so that it drops a server that stops
  within a second;
- passes the routes on to the other sites' routers, like your other
  routes, so that devices reach another site when theirs withdraws. A
  route from a router's own server (eBGP) already wins over the same
  route learned from another router (iBGP); don't give another site's
  route a higher local preference;
- doesn't send the anycast addresses elsewhere (a default route) while
  no server advertises them: a static route with a high distance can
  reject them.

The servers' AS: 65053. The firewall on each server lets its
routers in on TCP 179 (BGP) and UDP 3784 (BFD).

## east1-rtr (east1's site)

| Session | Router | Server | Server AS | Accept |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| IPv4 | 192.0.2.1 (AS 64500) | 192.0.2.31 | 65053 | 192.0.2.153/32 |
| IPv6 | 2001:db8:31::1 (AS 64500) | 2001:db8:31::31 | 65053 | 2001:db8:153::153/128 |

As FRR configuration, for example:

```text
ip prefix-list RADIUS-ANYCAST seq 10 permit 192.0.2.153/32
ipv6 prefix-list RADIUS-ANYCAST seq 10 permit 2001:db8:153::153/128
!
ip route 192.0.2.153/32 reject 250
ipv6 route 2001:db8:153::153/128 reject 250
!
route-map FROM-YUBIKEY permit 10
 match ip address prefix-list RADIUS-ANYCAST
exit
route-map FROM-YUBIKEY permit 20
 match ipv6 address prefix-list RADIUS-ANYCAST
exit
route-map NOTHING deny 10
exit
!
router bgp 64500
 neighbor 192.0.2.31 remote-as 65053
 neighbor 192.0.2.31 bfd
 neighbor 2001:db8:31::31 remote-as 65053
 neighbor 2001:db8:31::31 bfd
 address-family ipv4 unicast
  neighbor 192.0.2.31 activate
  neighbor 192.0.2.31 route-map FROM-YUBIKEY in
  neighbor 192.0.2.31 route-map NOTHING out
  neighbor 192.0.2.31 maximum-prefix 1
 exit-address-family
 address-family ipv6 unicast
  neighbor 2001:db8:31::31 activate
  neighbor 2001:db8:31::31 route-map FROM-YUBIKEY in
  neighbor 2001:db8:31::31 route-map NOTHING out
  neighbor 2001:db8:31::31 maximum-prefix 1
 exit-address-family
exit
```

## east2-rtr (east2's site)

| Session | Router | Server | Server AS | Accept |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| IPv4 | 198.51.100.1 (AS 64500) | 198.51.100.32 | 65053 | 192.0.2.153/32 |
| IPv6 | 2001:db8:32::1 (AS 64500) | 2001:db8:32::32 | 65053 | 2001:db8:153::153/128 |

As FRR configuration, for example:

```text
ip prefix-list RADIUS-ANYCAST seq 10 permit 192.0.2.153/32
ipv6 prefix-list RADIUS-ANYCAST seq 10 permit 2001:db8:153::153/128
!
ip route 192.0.2.153/32 reject 250
ipv6 route 2001:db8:153::153/128 reject 250
!
route-map FROM-YUBIKEY permit 10
 match ip address prefix-list RADIUS-ANYCAST
exit
route-map FROM-YUBIKEY permit 20
 match ipv6 address prefix-list RADIUS-ANYCAST
exit
route-map NOTHING deny 10
exit
!
router bgp 64500
 neighbor 198.51.100.32 remote-as 65053
 neighbor 198.51.100.32 bfd
 neighbor 2001:db8:32::32 remote-as 65053
 neighbor 2001:db8:32::32 bfd
 address-family ipv4 unicast
  neighbor 198.51.100.32 activate
  neighbor 198.51.100.32 route-map FROM-YUBIKEY in
  neighbor 198.51.100.32 route-map NOTHING out
  neighbor 198.51.100.32 maximum-prefix 1
 exit-address-family
 address-family ipv6 unicast
  neighbor 2001:db8:32::32 activate
  neighbor 2001:db8:32::32 route-map FROM-YUBIKEY in
  neighbor 2001:db8:32::32 route-map NOTHING out
  neighbor 2001:db8:32::32 maximum-prefix 1
 exit-address-family
exit
```

